1 #uuid "71a8ebde-d4a5-599a-bc49-21762635a32b" 13194139535116 true] [17592186332002 1034 "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}[{\\int }_{2{x}^{2}}^{1+{x}^{2}}f(x,y)\\text{ }dy]dx" 13194139535116 true] [17592186332002 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535116 true] [17592186332002 1111 #uuid "c4b10608-a519-5016-bcc8-d5e4a26f29c9" 13194139535116 true] [17592186332003 1034 "{\\int }_{1+{x}^{2}}^{2{x}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x,y)\\text{ }dy]dx" 13194139535116 true] [17592186332003 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535116 true] [17592186332003 1111 #uuid "8ab606c0-b89c-5943-b0fd-a198c82279d6" 13194139535116 true] [17592186332004 1017 #edb/ref 17592186332005 13194139535116 true] [17592186332004 1089 "q-40822" 13194139535116 true] [17592186332004 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535116 true] [17592186332004 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054607/assets/933e68e42fe61af3e30fae012b0b56dce6793b0fadc343e04a0766b85cb6ae7e.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y=9-{x}^{2}$ and $y=1+{x}^{2},$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}(x{y}^{2}+2x)\\,dA=$" 13194139535116 true] [17592186332004 1113 #uuid "26444f9e-45df-56c7-85ca-aa68ae575349" 13194139535116 true] [17592186332004 1145 "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. \n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{x}^{2} & =9-{x}^{2} \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x$-limits are \n$-2\\le x\\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054607/assets/c56c5be4ac88755f1859f295f700be8710d75b16380ac120e6b56a3aa767acbe.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,1+{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 9-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x{y}^{2}+2x)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{1+{x}^{2}}^{9-{x}^{2}}(x{y}^{2}+2x)\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535116 true] [17592186332005 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332006 13194139535116 true] [17592186332005 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332007 13194139535116 true] [17592186332005 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332008 13194139535116 true] [17592186332005 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332009 13194139535116 true] [17592186332005 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332010 13194139535116 true] [17592186332005 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535116 true] [17592186332005 1041 "selection" 13194139535116 true] [17592186332005 1104 #edb/ref 17592186332010 13194139535116 true] [17592186332005 1110 #uuid "49c33087-f456-55d0-8f3e-3f3fb3b75c94" 13194139535116 true] [17592186332006 1034 "{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x{y}^{2}+2x)\\,dy\\,dx" 13194139535116 true] [17592186332006 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535116 true] [17592186332006 1111 #uuid "e0f8aa78-f523-50db-a0dd-3011a3adb9c0" 13194139535116 true] [17592186332007 1034 "{\\int }_{1+{x}^{2}}^{2}{\\int }_{9-{x}^{2}}^{2}(x{y}^{2}+2x)\\,dy\\,dx" 13194139535116 true] [17592186332007 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535116 true] [17592186332007 1111 #uuid "b83f6f8f-0f79-5637-9484-1a57f6448678" 13194139535116 true] [17592186332008 1034 "{\\int }_{1+{x}^{2}}^{9-{x}^{2}}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}(x{y}^{2}+2x)\\,dy\\,dx" 13194139535116 true] [17592186332008 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535116 true] [17592186332008 1111 #uuid "c3407904-204f-5610-89a4-319e6c5675b8" 13194139535116 true] [17592186332009 1034 "{\\int }_{-2}^{9-{x}^{2}}{\\int }_{2}^{9-{x}^{2}}(x{y}^{2}+2x)\\,dy\\,dx" 13194139535116 true] [17592186332009 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535116 true] [17592186332009 1111 #uuid "c621dc57-07cd-57d8-88f8-f91cc5efa0f7" 13194139535116 true] [17592186332010 1034 "{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{1+{x}^{2}}^{9-{x}^{2}}(x{y}^{2}+2x)\\,dy\\,dx" 13194139535116 true] [17592186332010 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535116 true] [17592186332010 1111 #uuid "c0170f64-44a7-5ea7-a516-bb90f398b833" 13194139535116 true] [17592186332011 1017 #edb/ref 17592186332012 13194139535116 true] [17592186332011 1089 "q-40825" 13194139535116 true] [17592186332011 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535116 true] [17592186332011 1100 "If $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y=\\frac{1}{x},$ $y=\\sqrt{x},$ and $x=4,$ then ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA=$" 13194139535116 true] [17592186332011 1113 #uuid "db42cd1f-0714-52f4-b27c-88e397059aec" 13194139535116 true] [17592186332011 1145 "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054607/assets/fcb347767983c38eb12f521ca556dcc5a96705ee4e887fb8d43c42984dfe1a26.png)\n\nNow, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. \n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x} & =\\sqrt{x} \\\\ x\\sqrt{x} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot {(\\sqrt{x})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =1 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x$-limits are \n$1\\le x\\le 4.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054607/assets/91bf7087e5aa0053d24cb45158d48b0bb36fb84b28ebabaeba3c26dc62168dfe.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 4,\\,\\frac{1}{x}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{1/x}^{\\sqrt{x}}f(x,y)\\text{ }dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535116 true] [17592186332012 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332013 13194139535116 true] [17592186332012 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332014 131941395